1
00:00:12,214 --> 00:00:13,617
Good evening, everyone.

2
00:00:13,916 --> 00:00:15,660
I trust you've had a fruitful day.

3
00:00:15,679 --> 00:00:18,503
I do not say happy, though I hope it has been happy.

4
00:00:18,542 --> 00:00:27,335
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhodasa.

5
00:00:27,355 --> 00:00:34,965
Namo tassa bhagavato arahato sambha sambhodasa.

6
00:00:35,536 --> 00:00:44,530
Namo dasa bhagavato arahato sama sambuddhasa.

7
00:00:44,551 --> 00:00:51,904
Homage to the Buddha, the blessed, noble and fully self-awakened one.

8
00:00:51,963 --> 00:00:59,637
Today we ran a little day retreat for some people and I think one or two people will be here from that day, from today.

9
00:01:01,134 --> 00:01:18,353
And one thing that has come out with this lockdown and everything is how people have become neighbourly to help each other get food and stuff like that.

10
00:01:19,055 --> 00:01:23,379
And it's brought out the friendship which was hidden before.

11
00:01:24,441 --> 00:01:30,567
So I thought, well, today might be a good time to just look at one or two things the Buddha says about good friendship.

12
00:01:31,426 --> 00:01:42,588
This isn't from the Middle Lens sayings, these are taken from the Anguttanika, which is a collection of the discourses by numbers.

13
00:01:43,471 --> 00:01:46,397
One of these, two of those, right up to number 11.

14
00:01:47,305 --> 00:01:59,543
And also there's a quoting from the Dīgha Nikāya, that's the long length sayings, discourses, and it's from a discourse to Sīgālaka.

15
00:02:00,125 --> 00:02:03,609
It's a discourse which is devoted to lay people.

16
00:02:05,713 --> 00:02:08,638
And there's one here from the connected sayings.

17
00:02:09,225 --> 00:02:13,831
which is about friendship, about good friendship in the spiritual life.

18
00:02:14,252 --> 00:02:21,701
So my idea is that I will read out what the Buddha says about good friendship.

19
00:02:22,522 --> 00:02:25,486
And there are two sides to the question.

20
00:02:25,546 --> 00:02:32,735
So first of all, we have to bring somebody to mind or some people to mind who we consider to be good, close friends.

21
00:02:33,830 --> 00:02:43,258
Of course, your closest friend is, if you're married or you're in partnership, that would be your closest friend or your parents or whatever.

22
00:02:43,338 --> 00:02:48,182
But just to bring certain people to mind, and we're going to ask ourselves, would they?

23
00:02:48,203 --> 00:02:50,284
And then afterwards, would I?

24
00:02:50,625 --> 00:02:50,985
Okay.

25
00:02:51,825 --> 00:02:58,913
So the first one is, one should associate with a friend who possesses seven factors.

26
00:03:01,134 --> 00:03:03,616
They give what is hard to give.

27
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:09,525
So the first time round, it's somebody you know.

28
00:03:10,626 --> 00:03:11,387
Somebody who's a friend.

29
00:03:11,426 --> 00:03:15,312
Would they give something that is hard to give?

30
00:03:19,956 --> 00:03:24,162
Would they do something for you which was hard to do?

31
00:03:24,442 --> 00:03:26,844
In other words, asking a little sacrifice for that.

32
00:03:32,031 --> 00:03:32,912
Would they patiently...

33
00:03:33,431 --> 00:03:36,074
Endure what is hard to endure.

34
00:03:36,314 --> 00:03:37,635
In other words, when you're being nasty.

35
00:03:43,621 --> 00:03:46,063
Would they reveal their secrets to you?

36
00:03:46,903 --> 00:03:50,007
Would they give confidence like that?

37
00:03:56,752 --> 00:04:01,717
Would you have confidence in them not to tell your secrets to others?

38
00:04:08,818 --> 00:04:11,581
They would not forsake you when you're in trouble.

39
00:04:18,428 --> 00:04:20,370
They would not look down upon you.

40
00:04:21,831 --> 00:04:24,434
The word here is despise, but I think that's a bit strong.

41
00:04:30,060 --> 00:04:33,442
One should associate with a friend who possesses these seven factors.

42
00:04:33,744 --> 00:04:36,125
So now turning to ourselves, would we?

43
00:04:36,459 --> 00:04:38,824
towards certain people in our lives.

44
00:04:39,584 --> 00:04:43,071
Would we give what is hard to give?

45
00:04:49,281 --> 00:04:50,483
Think of an example, you see.

46
00:04:50,502 --> 00:04:55,310
It doesn't have to be just one person, but have you actually given what was hard to give?

47
00:04:59,177 --> 00:05:02,603
Have you done what was hard to do?

48
00:05:09,855 --> 00:05:12,139
Have you endured what was unpleasant?

49
00:05:16,127 --> 00:05:17,651
Unpleasant about them, that is.

50
00:05:24,805 --> 00:05:31,677
Have you revealed secrets to them that you wouldn't want other people to know, that is?

51
00:05:40,601 --> 00:05:43,725
And have you kept your confidentiality?

52
00:05:44,026 --> 00:05:45,927
Have you preserved their secrets?

53
00:05:59,221 --> 00:06:04,026
Have you ever forsaken somebody who was in trouble?

54
00:06:04,906 --> 00:06:06,168
You've just not helped them.

55
00:06:08,029 --> 00:06:08,870
When you could have done.

56
00:06:20,257 --> 00:06:23,446
Have you looked down upon friends on occasion?

57
00:06:29,019 --> 00:06:30,341
Or have you indulged that?

58
00:06:30,562 --> 00:06:37,338
I mean, occasionally maybe it happens, but have you actually indulged looking down upon them, thinking badly of them?

59
00:06:44,980 --> 00:06:50,649
So now there follows a verse, a friend gives what is hard to give and he does what is hard to do.

60
00:06:51,411 --> 00:06:56,059
They forgive you your harsh words and endure what is hard to endure.

61
00:06:57,141 --> 00:07:04,213
They tell you their secrets and they preserve your secrets.

62
00:07:04,233 --> 00:07:09,721
They do not forsake you in difficulties and they do not look down upon you.

63
00:07:11,507 --> 00:07:16,735
The person here in whom these qualities are found is a friend.

64
00:07:18,096 --> 00:07:24,845
One desiring a friend should resort to such a person.

65
00:07:24,865 --> 00:07:28,810
So that's the first description of a friend.

66
00:07:29,389 --> 00:07:33,795
The next one is, of course, they're all similar, but they do have a little twist.

67
00:07:36,798 --> 00:07:41,245
One should associate with a friend who possesses, again, seven qualities.

68
00:07:43,334 --> 00:07:45,278
They are pleasing and agreeable.

69
00:07:45,639 --> 00:07:46,661
That's an easy one, isn't it?

70
00:07:51,290 --> 00:07:52,992
They are respected.

71
00:07:55,036 --> 00:07:57,300
Somebody who's generally respected.

72
00:07:57,482 --> 00:08:00,146
They have to choose if you look around your friendships.

73
00:08:03,372 --> 00:08:04,836
They are esteemed.

74
00:08:09,557 --> 00:08:38,855
they are a speaker i tried to find out what exactly that means um doesn't i couldn't really find out what the meaning of that is and there's no notes on it so you'll just have to imagine what that means their speaker uh did they uh patiently endure being spoken to so that's when you have to say something which they don't like but is from your point of view they're good

75
00:08:39,746 --> 00:08:40,908
for their good?

76
00:08:47,240 --> 00:08:49,524
Do they have deep conversation?

77
00:08:57,318 --> 00:09:01,586
Do they not encourage you to do something which is wrong?

78
00:09:09,682 --> 00:09:38,727
so that's another set uh they're they're obviously very close together uh does does not in does not encourage your aim join you to do what is wrong so now what about us how do we um how have we acted in the past you see have we been pleasing and agreeable can we be respected

79
00:09:44,698 --> 00:09:46,160
Are we generally esteemed?

80
00:09:46,201 --> 00:09:48,224
In other words, are we well thought of?

81
00:09:54,491 --> 00:09:55,933
I don't know what that means.

82
00:09:56,094 --> 00:09:57,155
Are we a speaker?

83
00:09:58,256 --> 00:10:01,061
You'll have to imagine what that is.

84
00:10:04,666 --> 00:10:08,630
Do we patiently endure being spoken to, corrected?

85
00:10:14,246 --> 00:10:23,504
Do we talk deeply about things or at least go into things a little bit more than just surface talk?

86
00:10:30,960 --> 00:10:35,850
And do we not lead people into wrongdoings?

87
00:10:43,879 --> 00:10:46,023
So again, this is followed by a verse.

88
00:10:47,424 --> 00:10:53,313
They are dear, respected and esteemed, a speaker and one who endures speech.

89
00:10:54,274 --> 00:11:02,408
So I presume, not quite sure what that means, to give deep talks and does not enjoin one to do what is wrong.

90
00:11:03,609 --> 00:11:09,398
The person here in whom these qualities are found is a friend, benevolent and compassionate.

91
00:11:10,475 --> 00:11:39,216
even if one is dismissed by him one desiring a friend should resort to such a person so even if you meet somebody like that but they don't particularly want to know you we should try to find somebody similar so now there's a another another um way of describing four good friends four kinds this time it's four kinds of good friends and this is the advice he was giving to this man sigalaka

92
00:11:41,508 --> 00:12:06,687
so he says there are four kinds of kind-hearted friends so again if you can think of somebody the friend who is helpful a friend who shares one's happiness and suffering a friend who points out what is good

93
00:12:12,743 --> 00:12:14,365
and a friend who's sympathetic.

94
00:12:21,653 --> 00:12:22,813
Now what about ourselves?

95
00:12:22,874 --> 00:12:24,154
How do we see ourselves?

96
00:12:24,515 --> 00:12:25,437
Are we helpful?

97
00:12:31,403 --> 00:12:36,708
Do we share our happinesses and sufferings, disappointments and so on?

98
00:12:42,510 --> 00:12:44,374
Do we point to what is good?

99
00:12:51,803 --> 00:12:52,885
Are we sympathetic?

100
00:13:01,158 --> 00:13:04,001
And there are four cases of a helpful friend.

101
00:13:05,283 --> 00:13:07,826
They protect you when you are heedless.

102
00:13:09,932 --> 00:13:12,475
You think of somebody who's helped you when you're heedless.

103
00:13:17,620 --> 00:13:20,663
They look after your property when you're heedless.

104
00:13:21,325 --> 00:13:25,869
Not necessarily your house or anything, but whatever you've asked them to look after.

105
00:13:31,975 --> 00:13:34,118
Are they a refuge when you're frightened?

106
00:13:41,354 --> 00:13:46,280
And when your need has arisen, have they given twice the wealth required?

107
00:13:46,301 --> 00:13:49,124
That's a big much.

108
00:13:50,546 --> 00:13:52,908
In other words, have they given more than you asked for?

109
00:14:02,182 --> 00:14:02,883
What about us?

110
00:14:02,923 --> 00:14:09,792
Have we protected someone who is about to be heedless to do something which would have not looked good?

111
00:14:17,482 --> 00:14:21,227
Have we protected their property if they've been heedless?

112
00:14:31,302 --> 00:14:35,727
Have they been able to take refuge in us when they're frightened, anxious about something?

113
00:14:43,504 --> 00:14:48,312
And when somebody's asked for financial help, have we given them a little bit more?

114
00:14:59,048 --> 00:15:04,616
There are four cases of a friend who shares one's happiness and sufferings can be understood.

115
00:15:05,798 --> 00:15:07,461
So we've come across some of these.

116
00:15:08,261 --> 00:15:10,625
They reveal their secrets to you.

117
00:15:11,533 --> 00:15:13,716
They guard your secrets.

118
00:15:15,158 --> 00:15:17,360
They do not abandon you when you're in trouble.

119
00:15:17,379 --> 00:15:21,884
And this is the big one.

120
00:15:22,605 --> 00:15:25,568
They would sacrifice their life on your sake.

121
00:15:27,672 --> 00:15:31,936
Think of anybody who might sacrifice their life for you.

122
00:15:33,899 --> 00:15:35,279
It's a tall order, isn't it?

123
00:15:41,149 --> 00:15:41,850
What about us?

124
00:15:43,312 --> 00:15:46,336
So have we protected somebody when they're heedless?

125
00:15:47,298 --> 00:15:52,264
Sorry, have we shared somebody's happiness and suffering?

126
00:15:53,206 --> 00:15:59,573
Do we keep their secrets, guard their secrets?

127
00:16:00,215 --> 00:16:02,077
We've not abandoned them when in trouble.

128
00:16:02,118 --> 00:16:07,764
And this one, this final one, is there anyone whom we would sacrifice our life for?

129
00:16:17,043 --> 00:16:42,746
jesus of course said there is no greater gift than to give one's life for another and there have of course been cases in history uh the one that came to mind was more of a novel and the tale of two cities out of love i can't remember dalton is it um he finally goes to the guillotine uh replacing this man

130
00:16:43,148 --> 00:16:46,211
who his true love actually loved.

131
00:16:46,591 --> 00:16:47,352
She didn't love him.

132
00:16:49,414 --> 00:16:52,898
But he was happy to offer his life for her happiness.

133
00:16:54,539 --> 00:16:58,443
It's a far, far better thing I do now than I've ever done before.

134
00:17:00,346 --> 00:17:01,567
So that's a tough one, isn't it?

135
00:17:02,087 --> 00:17:03,649
Sacrifice your life for somebody.

136
00:17:06,593 --> 00:17:09,355
Four cases of a friend who points out what is good.

137
00:17:10,778 --> 00:17:11,198
So he...

138
00:17:11,329 --> 00:17:13,511
They restrain you from evil.

139
00:17:15,233 --> 00:17:16,976
So we've had something like that before.

140
00:17:17,477 --> 00:17:20,380
They enjoin you to do what is good.

141
00:17:21,320 --> 00:17:22,862
They encourage you to do what is good.

142
00:17:24,324 --> 00:17:27,047
They inform you of what you have not heard.

143
00:17:27,748 --> 00:17:38,298
So I presume it's if somebody said something about you, that they're happy to, they will inform you, especially if it's something that might harm you.

144
00:17:39,898 --> 00:17:42,303
And they point to you to the path of heaven.

145
00:17:42,363 --> 00:17:45,929
So that, of course, is the ethical path.

146
00:17:48,773 --> 00:17:51,459
There are four cases of a sympathetic friend.

147
00:17:52,661 --> 00:17:55,405
They do not rejoice in your misfortune.

148
00:17:56,607 --> 00:17:59,813
Can you think of somebody who does not rejoice in your misfortune?

149
00:18:03,398 --> 00:18:05,603
And they rejoice in your good fortune.

150
00:18:09,835 --> 00:18:12,921
They stop those who dispraise you.

151
00:18:18,210 --> 00:18:20,653
They commend those who speak praise of you.

152
00:18:27,807 --> 00:18:28,788
What about us?

153
00:18:32,869 --> 00:18:40,480
When a friend of ours is born to misfortune, do we rejoice or do we not rejoice?

154
00:18:40,500 --> 00:18:41,942
Can you think of an occasion?

155
00:18:47,788 --> 00:18:50,271
Do we rejoice in a friend's good fortune?

156
00:18:53,856 --> 00:18:57,020
Or are we caught up in jealousy or envy?

157
00:19:01,017 --> 00:19:06,321
Do you stop somebody who's speaking badly about your friend?

158
00:19:13,769 --> 00:19:15,852
And commend those who speak well of them?

159
00:19:22,278 --> 00:19:24,279
So these are all little pointers.

160
00:19:25,381 --> 00:19:28,964
But the final one also is about spiritual friendships.

161
00:19:29,636 --> 00:19:31,080
So here's Ananda.

162
00:19:31,181 --> 00:19:35,571
Ananda always says that he knows something and then the Buddha always has to correct him.

163
00:19:35,932 --> 00:19:40,103
So here's Ananda saying, Bhante, this is half the spiritual life.

164
00:19:40,488 --> 00:19:44,857
That is good friendship, good companionship and good comradeship.

165
00:19:44,877 --> 00:19:48,786
So now this is about what you need for your spiritual life.

166
00:19:48,806 --> 00:20:00,571
So this isn't about your work or your finances or anything, but in what way is good friendship part of your spiritual endeavour?

167
00:20:01,852 --> 00:20:04,714
So he says it's only half the spiritual life.

168
00:20:05,135 --> 00:20:08,078
So the Buddha says, no, not so Ananda, not so.

169
00:20:08,500 --> 00:20:11,303
This is the entire spiritual life, Ananda.

170
00:20:11,963 --> 00:20:15,989
That is good friendship, good companionship, good comradeship.

171
00:20:16,930 --> 00:20:26,661
When a person has a good friend, a good companion, a good comrade, it is expected that they will develop and cultivate the Noble Eightfold Path.

172
00:20:27,198 --> 00:20:33,928
So having a good friend on the spiritual path is obviously going to be a great support.

173
00:20:35,009 --> 00:20:46,406
I mean, I've known cases where one person within a marriage became a Buddhist and the other one just couldn't stand it really, found it very threatening.

174
00:20:46,867 --> 00:20:48,169
It was a very difficult time.

175
00:20:48,189 --> 00:20:51,213
I mean, eventually it sorted itself out.

176
00:20:52,020 --> 00:21:01,272
But if you happen to be in a relationship where both of you are committed to the path, what you can see, that would be a great support.

177
00:21:09,964 --> 00:21:15,732
So it just ends off by saying it may be understood how the entire spiritual life

178
00:21:15,950 --> 00:21:37,519
His good friendship, good companionship, good comradeship, by relying, and then he talks about himself, you see, that confidence that we should have in him, by relying upon me as a good friend, being subject to birth, are freed from birth, being subjects to old age, are freed from old age, or from aging, that's better.

179
00:21:38,089 --> 00:21:40,434
Being subject to death are freed from death.

180
00:21:40,575 --> 00:21:51,175
Being subject to sorrow, lamentation, pain, dejection and despair are freed from sorrow, lamentation, pain, dejection and despair.

181
00:21:51,496 --> 00:21:54,883
By this method to ananda it may be understood

182
00:21:54,863 --> 00:22:00,173
how the entire spiritual life is good friendship, good companionship and good comradeship.

183
00:22:00,474 --> 00:22:08,771
So in other words, when we take refuge in the Buddha and his teaching, they become our very good spiritual companions.

184
00:22:09,967 --> 00:22:36,092
so uh these are just reflections it's a way that we can use the um the discourses just to consider ourselves and to move us along the path i can only hope that you are moving along the path and that you will be soon that you will be liberated from all suffering sooner rather than later so we can begin our practice

